# Evening on the Tsour-Vai

2026-11-08

The boatman Aulus Phremm took on his passenger at the town of Belcaster, some four hundred leagues above the delta, on an afternoon in the month of Hessel.¹ The passenger was a young woman in a plain traveling-cloak who gave her name as Irien and declined to give any other particular about herself, and who paid the fare for passage to the town of Low Belling in advance, in good Tsour-Vaian coin, without haggling. Aulus Phremm, who had been on the river forty years and had carried every sort of passenger a river can carry, asked no questions. He helped her aboard, stowed her small bundle beneath the forward thwart, and pushed off into the current.

The boat was a long narrow craft of the kind particular to the Tsour-Vai, called a sillen, broad enough for three passengers abreast and rigged with a single small sail for the upstream returns. Aulus Phremm poled from the stern in the slow water and let the current do the work in the fast, and the banks passed on either side in their long slow green procession.

The first tower they passed was at the village of Ossel-by-the-Ford, a squat gray tower of seven bells, its stone weathered smooth by six hundred years of river-damp. Irien sat in the bow and watched it slide by without turning her head.

“You will want to look at the towers, miss,” said Aulus Phremm. “They are what people come for.”

“I have seen towers.”

“Not these towers.”

“Towers are towers.”

Aulus Phremm poled in silence for a while. The sun began to decline. They passed the tower at Irrevil, which had eleven bells arranged in two tiers, and the tower at Pont-Hausser, which was famous for a bronze bell of exceptional sweetness cast in the reign of the Fourth Custodian.² Irien did not turn her head.

At length Aulus Phremm said, “You are in some trouble.”

“Why do you say that?”

“Because a person not in trouble looks at the towers. It is a thing I have observed.”

Irien said nothing for a while. The river made its small sounds against the hull. At last she said, “I am going to Low Belling because my sister is there, and because I cannot go back to Belcaster, and because I have nowhere else to go. Is that trouble enough for you, boatman?”

“It is ordinary trouble,” said Aulus Phremm. “The river carries much of it. I meant no prying.”

“I know. I am sorry.”

“There is no need.”

They went on. The sun lowered toward the western bank. Aulus Phremm, who had timed the journey, as he always did, to bring them through the great bend at Carissel at the hour of bells, guided the sillen into the slack water on the inside of the bend and let it drift. The bend was broad and slow, and from this point one could see, looking both up and down the river, some thirty towers at once.

“We will rest here a little,” he said. “The current will carry us well enough.”

“I am in no hurry.”

“No. You would not be, going to Low Belling.”

She looked at him then. “Have you been there?”

“Many times. It is a quiet town.”

“My sister says it is dull.”

“It is. Dullness is not the worst thing a town can be.”

“No,” said Irien. “No, it is not.”

The sun touched the western ridge. In the tower at Carissel, which stood on a small promontory overlooking the bend, a bell-ringer whom Aulus Phremm could not see but had seen many times, an old woman named Phelma Ossent who had been ringing the Carissel bells for fifty-one years, took her place at the ropes. In towers up and down the river, men and women and occasionally children did the same. They watched the sun together, across the leagues, and waited for the signal which was the sun’s lower limb touching the ridge, and then each of them, in their own tower, began.

The first bell to ring was at Carissel, because the Carissel tower stood slightly west of its neighbors and caught the signal first. It was a single low note, not loud, and it hung in the air for a long moment alone. Then the tower at Irrevil, half a league upstream, answered with three notes of its own. Then Pont-Hausser with its famous bronze bell. Then the small two-bell tower at Nethering, and the great fourteen-bell tower at Belissar, and the tower at Ossel-by-the-Ford where they had passed an hour before, and then, in a cascade that overtook itself, every tower within hearing of every other tower, each ringing its own particular peal in its own particular time, the peals overlapping and reinforcing and modulating one another until the air above the river was a single vast slow-moving fabric of sound, dense and harmonic and moving downstream faster than the current and upstream faster than the wind.

Irien stood up in the sillen. She had not meant to; she only found she had. The boat rocked slightly and Aulus Phremm steadied it with a practiced hand.

The cascade reached them in full. They were in the middle of it now, the towers all around, and the sound came from every direction at once, and Irien closed her eyes and stood very still in the rocking boat, and after some time Aulus Phremm saw that she was weeping, without sound, the way a person weeps who has not expected to.

The bells rang their full round, which takes a quarter of an hour, and then one by one they ceased, in the reverse of the order in which they had begun, the easternmost towers falling silent first and the silence moving upstream as the sound had moved down, until at last only the Carissel tower was ringing, and then Carissel too fell silent, and the river was quiet again except for the small sounds of the water.

Irien sat down. She wiped her face with the sleeve of her cloak.

“I did not know,” she said.

“No one does, who has not heard it.”

“My sister has not told me.”

“Your sister has lived in Low Belling some time, I think. One forgets, after some time. The bells become ordinary. This is perhaps the saddest thing about them, but it is true.”

“I shall remember.”

“You may. Some do. Most do not.”

Aulus Phremm took up his pole and eased the sillen back into the current. The light was failing now and he wanted to reach the night-mooring at Shelf-Farrow before full dark. Irien sat in the bow and watched the towers as they passed, and this time she turned her head for each one.

They reached Low Belling on the afternoon of the third day. Irien’s sister was waiting at the wharf, a thin woman in a blue dress who embraced her without speaking and took her bundle and led her away up the stone steps toward the town. Irien turned at the top of the steps and raised one hand to Aulus Phremm, and he raised his pole in answer, and then she was gone.

He stayed three days in Low Belling, waiting for a return cargo, and on the evening of the second day he walked up to the Low Belling tower, which was a modest tower of five bells, and stood in the small square below it at the hour of bells, to see how they rang it there. They rang it well enough. It was not Carissel. No tower was Carissel, to Aulus Phremm, who had spent forty years listening from the middle of the river. But it was well enough.

Walking back to his boat in the dusk, he passed a small house on a side street, and through the open window he saw Irien sitting at a table with her sister, a lamp lit between them, bread and a dish of something on the board, their heads bent toward each other in conversation. He did not stop. He went on to his boat and slept aboard, and in the morning he took on a cargo of cured fish and Low Belling lacquer-ware and began the long slow journey upstream toward Belcaster, which would take him, against the current, some eighteen days.

On every one of those eighteen evenings, wherever he happened to be on the river, he stopped poling at the hour of bells and let the sillen drift, and he listened.

Notes

1. The Tsour-Vaian calendar divides the year into eight months of roughly equal length, named for the prevailing agricultural or riverine activity of each: Sowing, Flood, Rise, Hessel (a word of disputed etymology, perhaps referring to the hazy light of high summer), Harvest, Fall, Low-Water, and Rest. The bells are rung in all seasons, but their tone is held to vary with the humidity of the air, so that a connoisseur of bells can tell Sowing from Hessel with his eyes closed, or claims he can.

2. The Custodians are the traditional civic authorities of the major Tsour-Vaian towns, an office of considerable antiquity and little present power, the actual administration of the towns having passed some centuries ago to elected councils. A Custodian today is largely a ceremonial figure, presiding at festivals, commissioning new bells when the old ones crack, and keeping the town’s ancient register, which in some towns goes back nearly two thousand years and records, among other things, the name of every bell-ringer who has ever held the office there. The register of Carissel, kept in a stone chest in the base of the tower, is held to be the most complete on the river, though the claim is disputed by Belissar and by Pont-Hausser.

